In this lesson, we'll look at how areas and volumes change when you scale a shape up or down. Here's the big idea. Pick one shape, then make a bigger copy where every single length is multiplied by the same number. That number is called the linear scale factor, let's call it k. Now, here's where it gets satisfying. You don't have to measure every face or edge again. If lengths are scaled by k, then areas are always scaled by k squared, and volumes are always scaled by k cubed. So say k is two, meaning you've doubled every length. The new area isn't two times bigger, it's four times bigger, because you doubled in two directions. And the new volume isn't four or even six times bigger, it's eight times bigger, because you doubled in three directions. Quick sanity check. A three by four rug has an area of twelve. Double each side, so k equals two, and the new rug has area forty-eight, which is exactly four times twelve. That's k squared, not k cubed. Areas go with the square, volumes go with the cube. Now let's see it in action.